WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.660
- Can you even remember a
time before social media,

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.110
you actually had to talk to somebody

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:06.720
to see how they were doing.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.350
- If you wanted to know what was trending,

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:10.210
you'd have to go down to the mall,

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:11.690
check out the new styles

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:12.950
- And arguing with your uncle

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:16.420
about politics only
happened at family reunions.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:17.450
Thank goodness

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.480
- You might also have had
a little bit more money

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.970
in your pocket because of
social media's influence

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.540
on how we spent in a recent survey.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:28.500
57% of millennials said
they overspent their budget

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.240
because of something
they saw on social media.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.780
These tech companies have made
it possible for us to stay

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.500
in touch with friends and
family, like never before

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.980
but they've also created an
incredible marketing tool.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:44.734
Perfectly engineered to part
you from your hard earned cash.

00:00:44.734 --> 00:00:47.901
(piano music playing)

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:56.310
- Social media is such a
constant part of our lives.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:59.870
It's often hard to step
back and see the many ways

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:01.800
it affects our spending habits.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.980
So let's start with the most
obvious one. Advertising.

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:10.390
- Since ye old shopkeeper posted
a flyer in the town square,

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:13.560
advertising has become a
centuries long arms race

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:16.740
to capture consumers, eyes and dollars.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.460
If that flyer was a primitive spear

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:23.740
the internet is a laser guided
nuclear tipped smart ball.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.900
Unlike old fashioned
advertising that relied

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.180
on broad demographics,
like age, race, and income

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:32.250
today's digital marketers
can create detailed profiles

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.210
of each consumer tracking their interests

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.720
behaviors and emotional state.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:41.490
- Social media has made this
process ultra efficient.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:43.670
By analyzing what posts you like

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:45.260
what locations you've visited

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:49.520
and even what words you type
algorithms can assemble a slate

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.940
of ads, custom tailored for you.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:54.300
Well, what's wrong with
that? You might ask.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.200
Better to show me things I
want than things I don't,

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.270
but remember the point
of advertising is not

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.750
and has never been to
get you to spend money

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:05.980
on things you need or things
that will make you happy.

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:09.930
But just to spend money period,
the algorithms don't care

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.690
whether you can afford it
or whether you'll regret it.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:15.400
Their only job is to get
you to buy something.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:19.350
Anything. Now they're
experts on human psychology

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:22.090
and they know your specific
habits and weaknesses.

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:24.110
It's not a fair fight

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:28.030
- But the real cutting edge
stuff is branded content

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:30.150
where the line between advertising

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:34.280
and entertainment is so blurred
it's basically non-existent.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:35.700
Back in the eighties and nineties

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.580
the TV networks used to
show these weird bumpers

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.362
in between cartoons and commercials.

00:02:41.362 --> 00:02:44.651
- (TV playing) After these
messages we'll be right back ♪

00:02:44.651 --> 00:02:46.160
(whistling)

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.090
- I didn't think much
about it at the time,

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:50.570
but those bumpers were actually mandated

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:53.670
by the federal communications
commission because they worry

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:56.690
that children cannot
distinguish conceptually

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:59.130
between programming and advertising.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:02.260
Keep in mind, there was a
time when people questioned

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:04.960
the morality of advertising
to children at all.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:07.410
So that was considered a compromise.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:09.610
You know, if you're going
to manipulate underdeveloped

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:13.250
brains with visual stimuli
and advanced psychology,

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.640
at least give a little tykes a heads up.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.930
- That concern seems
positively quaint today.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:21.910
Modern brands produce
their own viral videos.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.670
They have social media
accounts that get likes

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:26.800
and follows just like real people.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:30.760
And they pay influencers to
seamlessly weave their products

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:32.100
into their posts.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.820
There are some loose rules around labeling

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:37.610
but the general idea is
to make the experience

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:40.300
of watching it ad indistinguishable

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.650
from watching actual content.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:44.300
And it's working

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:45.380
- In a recent study.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:48.510
Researchers found that
gen Z audiences were far

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:52.710
more receptive to branded content
than previous generations.

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:55.270
They're more likely to
check out branded photos

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:58.770
like and share ads and even
tag their friends in them.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:01.930
They actually prefer their
advertising to be a part

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:04.300
of the content than separate from it.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.870
- And Hey, that's you no judgment

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.980
but you should be clear
about the relationship.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:13.210
These ads may feel no different
from authentic content

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:16.420
but unlike a musician
artist or other creator

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.730
whose goal it is to make
you laugh, dance cry

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:24.370
or think brands are only
after one thing. Your money.

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:25.840
That is the dark truth.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:28.760
They worked so hard to make you forget.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:30.680
- It didn't have to be this way.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:33.010
Social media platforms
could have adopted a

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:35.570
subscription model like
Netflix or Spotify,

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:39.350
where you the customer pay
for access to their services.

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:41.970
But sites like Facebook,
Twitter and Instagram

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:45.140
instead decided it
would be more profitable

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:48.700
to treat their advertisers
as the real customers.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:52.010
And the product they sell them, is you.

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:55.680
- But we're not entirely
blameless in this process

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.300
whether we know it or not,

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:01.010
most users of social media
are encouraging each other

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:03.040
to spend more money.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.910
In the early part of the 20th century,

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:08.680
there was a popular comic strip
about a family who obsessed

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:11.220
with their social status was always trying

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:14.640
to match the fashionable
lifestyle of their neighbors.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.010
The title of the strip became synonymous

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:21.260
with conspicuous consumption,
driven by social pressure.

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:23.400
Keeping up with the Joneses.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.100
If the Jonesses is get a new car,

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:26.850
we feel like we need to get one.

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:29.400
If they put in a pool,
darn it. We should too.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.130
We don't want to be left behind.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:34.430
- Today, We might know this
phenomenon by another name.

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:37.840
FOMO. Our Instagram feeds
are filled with reminders

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:41.080
that everyone else is out
there living their best lives.

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:43.070
Shopping at the trendiest boutiques,

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:46.360
staying at five star hotels,
eating at gourmet restaurants,

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:50.190
sitting in the VIP section
of the hottest music clubs.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:51.570
We're not just comparing ourselves

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:53.440
to our immediate neighbors.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:56.800
We're comparing ourselves
to everyone all the time.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.560
And the only way not to feel left behind

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:02.188
is to get out there and drop some coin.

00:06:02.188 --> 00:06:03.021
(plates breaking)

00:06:03.021 --> 00:06:04.970
- The problem is that
your Instagram friends

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:08.340
aren't that much more
trustworthy than the advertisers.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:10.070
We don't admit to each other

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:13.110
that the lobster at Luigi's
was actually kind of dry

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:15.740
and we wish we hadn't
spent 75 bucks on it.

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:19.020
Or that those $300 pumps
from uptown gave us a blister

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:21.050
and we haven't worn them since October.

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:23.230
And we certainly don't share the fact

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:25.750
that we missed a payment
on our credit card.

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:28.180
Our desire to present our own lives

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:31.990
as perfect can turn us
into unwitting salespeople

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:34.450
for every product and service we use.

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:38.230
Whether or not we were actually
satisfied with the purchase.

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:41.500
- Even if social media makes
you feel better about yourself,

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:44.720
it can still have a
negative financial effect.

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:47.360
A study of Facebook
users in 2012 found out

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:50.190
that people who got a
strong, positive feedback

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:53.550
from their online social network
tended to be more reckless

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:56.460
with money and less
focused on their goals.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:00.990
Basically feeling popular
online can inflate your ego

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:02.680
and make you careless.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:05.490
- You don't even have to
make a post or write a review

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:07.580
to influence someone else's spending.

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:11.020
Just liking or following
a brand will do the trick.

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:14.700
Humans don't like to be the
first to try something new.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.870
We need to know that a product or brand

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:19.530
has already been accepted by the group

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:21.290
before we spend our money on it.

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:22.940
It's called social proof.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:25.060
And it's the reason why companies try

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:28.240
to accumulate followers
and likes on social media.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:31.130
They know that without
any other information,

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:33.730
consumers will go with
whatever seems to be

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:36.030
the most popular option.

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:38.890
Advertisers used to have
to fake social proof

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:40.610
by making commercials filled

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:43.590
with ordinary people using their product.

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:46.950
But now they can actually
show you the numbers.

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:50.430
See, can 105 million people be wrong?

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:54.410
- All of these tools will prime
a customer for a purchase.

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:57.330
But social media has
one more powerful trick

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:59.590
up its sleeve to seal the deal.

00:07:59.590 --> 00:08:02.610
A seamless shopping experience.

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:04.720
In the old days, you might see a TV ad

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:08.150
for a razor scooter and
think, Hey, that looks cool.

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:10.160
And then your friends at
the skate park tell you

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:11.480
how much they love theirs.

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:13.440
And you've made up your mind to buy one

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:15.800
with your birthday money, but
you still have to get your mom

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:18.180
to take you to sharper
image before they sell out.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:20.020
And by the time they get new ones in stock

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:21.360
everyone has moved on to Heelys

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:23.390
and your life is ruined.

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:25.870
- Babe, you got to let it go.

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:29.740
Today, If a social media
influencer shows off a new handbag

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:31.470
or visits a fancy resort,

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:33.840
there's often a link
to where you can buy it

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.910
or book tickets instantly.

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:38.140
Marketers know that a lot can happen

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:41.070
between the ad and the sale
and they want to hurry you

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:44.350
through checkout while
you're still full of FOMO.

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:47.510
While I love how easy the
internet has made shopping

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:50.100
it can be a curse for people who struggle

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:52.090
with impulse purchases.

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:54.210
- For all the talk about how social media

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:57.780
lets us express ourselves
and show our individuality.

00:08:57.780 --> 00:08:59.870
It's also revealed how susceptible

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:01.670
we are to social pressure.

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:03.370
- We trust messages more,

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:05.720
when we think they come from friends.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:08.910
We tend to follow the herd
when we don't know what to do,

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:10.380
and we'll do anything

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:13.770
to avoid feeling left out or left behind.

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:15.970
Modern marketers, know these weaknesses

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:18.960
in a figured out ways
to use them against us.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:21.010
Which can make social media hazardous

00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:22.270
to your financial health.

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:26.330
- But social media also offers
consumers more information

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:28.090
than they've ever had before.

00:09:28.090 --> 00:09:30.540
Finding deals and comparison Shopping

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:32.460
can be just a few clicks away.

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:34.630
And you can find lots of great advice

00:09:34.630 --> 00:09:37.060
from financial groups and channels like,

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:38.650
oh! I don't know this one.

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:41.150
So if you're going to
keep scrolling and sharing

00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:42.730
give us a like, or a follow

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:45.220
so we can help you stay balanced.

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:47.030
And that's our 2 cents.

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:48.440
- Thanks to our patrons

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:51.790
for keeping 2 cents financially
healthy, click the link

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:55.286
in the description to
become a 2 cents patron.

00:09:55.286 --> 00:09:56.670
(Gentle music playing)

00:09:56.670 --> 00:09:57.810
- If you want to know more

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:00.580
about how advertisers follow
you through cyberspace

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:04.236
check out our video, "How
Retailers Stalk You Online."

00:10:04.236 --> 00:10:07.486
(Gentle music playing)

